 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dblk0Wqpbap/",
 "title": "Why Top Performers Get Smaller Raises Than New Hires",
 "topic": "mindset",
 "summary": "The creator shares a personal management experience where her top-performing employee received a 3% raise while a new hire received 18%. The creator claims companies often reward retention and risk rather than performance, leaving loyal employees penalized.",
 "key_points": [
  "The creator's top-performing employee received a 3% raise because she was already at the top of her pay band.",
  "A new hire who had been there for six months received an 18% raise because a competitor made an offer and retention flagged him as a flight risk.",
  "The creator claims companies don't reward performance; they respond to risk.",
  "If you are not a flight risk, the creator claims you are not a priority in budget decisions.",
  "The creator offers a $22 program called the promotion sprint for scripts and resources to help people get promoted."
 ],
 "content": "Post text: This broke my heart. 💔\nTranscript: I gave my best employee a 3% raise. The new guy got 18%. I had to look my best employee in the eye and tell her she got a 3% raise, knowing that the guy next to her got 18%. And it broke my heart. When you become a manager, they don't warn you about this part. You sit in a room with HR and finance and they hand you a spreadsheet. Here's what each person on your team is getting. And you realize in that moment that it was never a conversation, it was always pre-decided. My top performer, she stayed late, picked up every project no one else wanted, had the best numbers on the team. 3%? Why? Because she was already at the top of her pay band. Meanwhile, the new hire who'd been there six months, they bumped him up to 18% because a competitor made an offer and retention flagged him as a flight risk. Same team, same review cycle, wildly different outcomes. And neither one had anything to do with performance. The worst part, I couldn't tell her. I had to sit across from her, read the script HR gave me, and say, we really value your contributions. And I watched her jaw drop. She thought she wasn't good enough. She wasn't, she was too good, and too loyal, and the system punished her for it. Companies don't reward performance. Maybe only a little bit. They respond to risk. And if you're not a flight risk, you're not a priority. And that's not cynical. That's just how the budget works, and no one tells you that until it's way too late. If you've ever been that loyal employee who gets the smallest raise, it wasn't you. Tell me your story in the comments. And if you're ready to create real visibility and impact within your organization and position yourself for your next promotion, comment sprint below and join my $22 program, the promotion sprint where I share with you scripts, frameworks, tools and resources to help you get promoted this quarter.\nOn screen text: I gave my best employee a 3% raise. The new guy got 18%. 💔",
